insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('ET01', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law', 'Conflicting legal standards', 'A member practices in a country whose securities laws are less strict than the CFA Institute Code of Ethics and Standards of Professional Conduct on a specific point. Under Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law, the member is required to follow:', '["Whichever of the applicable law or the Code and Standards imposes the stricter requirement","The local law only, since members must always defer to the law of the country where they practice","The Code and Standards only, since they always supersede any applicable law or regulation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard I(A) requires a member to comply with whichever of applicable law or regulation and the Code and Standards is stricter on a given point, so the Code''s stricter requirement controls here despite the more permissive local law. Deferring to local law is often correct when it is at least as strict as the Code, which makes that answer tempting, but it is not an unconditional rule; the opposite claim, that the Code always overrides law, is equally tempting since the Code is frequently the stricter source, but the Code does not automatically supersede a stricter law either, since Standard I(A) is a case-by-case comparison rather than a fixed hierarchy.'),
  ('ET02', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(A) Knowledge of the Law', 'Dissociating from violations', 'An analyst at Marrow Creek Partners discovers that a colleague has been altering client account statements to conceal poor performance. The analyst reports the conduct to compliance, then continues to help the colleague prepare those same client statements each month, believing the report alone satisfies her ethical duty. Under Standard I(A), her continued participation in preparing the statements is:', '["Acceptable, because reporting suspected misconduct to compliance is the only obligation Standard I(A) imposes","Still a problem, because a member who becomes aware of a violation she cannot stop must dissociate from the activity, which reporting alone does not accomplish while she keeps helping prepare the same statements","Only a violation of Standard III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality, since the issue concerns client account information"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard I(A) requires a member who is aware of a violation of laws, rules, or regulations to disassociate from the activity; escalating to compliance is good practice but does not by itself satisfy the duty if the member keeps participating in preparing the very statements being falsified. Treating the report as sufficient on its own is tempting because escalating a suspected violation is indeed part of the expected response, but Standard I(A) goes further and requires actually removing herself from continued involvement. Standard III(E) is a plausible-sounding alternative because client statements are involved, but III(E) governs keeping client information confidential from outsiders, not a colleague''s internal falsification of that information.'),
  ('ET03', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity', 'Issuer-paid travel and gifts', 'A publicly traded company invites a sell-side analyst who covers its stock to a three-day product launch event at a luxury resort, with the company paying for airfare, lodging, and all meals. The analyst''s employer has no policy addressing such invitations. Under Standard I(B), the most appropriate course of action for the analyst is to:', '["Attend as invited, since accepting hospitality from a covered company is normal industry practice and requires no further action","Decline the invitation entirely, since Standard I(B) prohibits a member from having any contact at all with a covered company''s management","Pay for her own travel and lodging where practical, or at minimum disclose the company-paid travel to her employer, so the benefit cannot be seen as compromising her independence and objectivity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard I(B) requires members to maintain independence and objectivity by refusing gifts, benefits, or entertainment that could reasonably be expected to compromise their own or their firm''s independence, and lavish, fully paid travel from a company under active coverage is exactly this kind of benefit, so the analyst should pay her own way where practical or at minimum disclose the arrangement so its influence is managed rather than hidden. Treating this as normal industry practice ignores that the size and source of the benefit are exactly what triggers the Standard, regardless of how common such invitations are. Declining all contact with the company overcorrects, since Standard I(B) targets compromising gifts and entertainment, not the ordinary engagement with management that covering analysts are expected to maintain.'),
  ('ET04', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity', 'Issuer-paid research', 'A small-cap company pays an independent research firm a flat fee to produce equity research coverage on its own stock, a common arrangement for companies too small to attract traditional sell-side coverage. For the research firm''s analysts to comply with Standard I(B), this issuer-paid arrangement must:', '["Be disclosed, with the report''s conclusions not influenced by the fact that the subject company is paying for the coverage","Never be entered into at all, because Standard I(B) prohibits any research arrangement in which the subject of the report also pays for it","Be kept confidential from clients, because disclosing the fee arrangement to clients would itself violate the issuer''s rights under Standard III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Issuer-paid research is permitted under Standard I(B) provided the compensation arrangement is disclosed and does not compromise the independence and objectivity of the actual analysis. A blanket prohibition is tempting because being paid by the very company one is analyzing sounds inherently compromised, but the Standard permits the arrangement with proper disclosure and safeguards rather than banning it outright. Standard III(E) is a real Standard but misapplied here: it protects client confidential information, not an issuer''s payment arrangement, and hiding the fee source from clients is backwards, since it is clients who need that disclosure to evaluate potential bias, not the issuer who needs confidentiality.'),
  ('ET05', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(C) Misrepresentation', 'Guaranteeing performance', 'A financial advisor tells a prospective client that she personally guarantees the client will earn at least 8% annually by investing with the firm, no matter what the market does. This statement is:', '["Permissible, provided the firm''s historical average return has in fact exceeded 8% annually","A violation of Standard I(C) Misrepresentation, because guaranteeing a specific investment return misrepresents the nature of investment performance, which cannot be assured","A violation of Standard III(C) Suitability, because the advisor has not yet assessed whether an 8% target is appropriate for this particular client"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard I(C) prohibits misrepresenting facts, including guaranteeing investment performance; returns on ordinary investments cannot be assured, so a blanket promise of a fixed return regardless of market conditions is a clear misrepresentation. A strong historical average is tempting because it might make the number seem achievable, but past results never justify guaranteeing a future return, which is the exact misrepresentation the Standard targets. Suitability is a real Standard, but it governs whether a recommendation fits a particular client''s circumstances, a question that has not even been reached yet since no client-specific recommendation appears; the problem here is the nature of the promise itself, not whether it fits this client.'),
  ('ET06', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(C) Misrepresentation', 'Plagiarism', 'An equity analyst at Kestrel Investment Group copies several paragraphs of macroeconomic analysis word for word from a widely circulated independent research report and includes them in her own published report without attribution, presenting the analysis as her own work. This is a violation of:', '["Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information, because the research was based on information not available to the general public","Standard V(C) Record Retention, because the analyst failed to retain documentation of the original source","Standard I(C) Misrepresentation, because presenting another party''s work as one''s own is plagiarism regardless of whether the underlying research was publicly available"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard I(C) prohibits plagiarism, meaning the use of another party''s work or ideas without attribution presented as one''s own, regardless of whether the underlying material was publicly available. Standard II(A) is a real Standard but misapplied, since the source report was widely circulated and therefore public, so there is no confidential information to have improperly traded on or misused. Standard V(C) is also real but misdirected, since it concerns retaining records supporting one''s own research and recommendations, not the separate problem of failing to credit another author''s published work.'),
  ('ET07', 'Ethics', 'Standard I(D) Misconduct', 'Personal dishonesty and integrity', 'A portfolio manager, in his personal capacity and unrelated to his job duties, is convicted of intentionally submitting falsified documents to obtain a personal loan. His employer and clients are unaware of the conviction, and no client funds or firm business were involved in any way. Under Standard I(D), this conduct:', '["Can still constitute a violation, because Standard I(D) covers conduct involving fraud or deceit that reflects adversely on a member''s professional reputation, integrity, or competence, even when it occurs outside of professional activities","Cannot constitute a violation, because Standard I(D) applies only to misconduct committed in the course of professional investment activities","Cannot constitute a violation, because no client or employer was harmed by the conduct"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard I(D) is not limited to professional activities; it also reaches personal conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, or deceit when that conduct reflects adversely on the member''s professional reputation, integrity, or competence, and knowingly submitting falsified loan documents is exactly this kind of dishonesty. Limiting the Standard to on-the-job conduct is the most common misreading of I(D), tempting because most other Standards do tie directly to professional or investment activities, but I(D) is a deliberate exception reaching serious personal dishonesty as well. The absence of client or employer harm is also tempting since many ethics violations do turn on harm to an identifiable party, but I(D) turns on the character of the conduct itself and its reflection on integrity, not on whether a victim suffered a measurable loss.'),
  ('ET08', 'Ethics', 'Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'Mosaic theory', 'An analyst combines several pieces of public information, including published financial statements, industry trade data, and a competitor''s public earnings call, with her own observation that a company''s parking lot has appeared unusually full on recent site visits, and concludes the company will likely beat its next earnings estimate. She has not obtained any confidential information from the company itself. Publishing a report with this conclusion:', '["Violates Standard II(A), because combining an inference with public data to predict earnings is only permitted for information the company has directly disclosed","Does not violate Standard II(A), because the mosaic theory permits combining public information and nonmaterial, non-confidential observations into a conclusion, even one that would be material if it had come from an inside source","Violates Standard II(A), because any earnings prediction made before the company''s official announcement is inherently based on material nonpublic information"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mosaic theory holds that an analyst may combine public information with nonmaterial, non-confidential observations, such as a parking lot''s fullness, to reach a conclusion, even one that would itself be material if it had instead come from an inside source with access to actual confidential figures. Requiring company disclosure before any inference can be drawn is tempting because it sounds cautious, but it would eliminate independent research entirely rather than target only improperly sourced research. Timing before the earnings release feels suspicious, which makes the blanket any-pre-announcement-prediction-is-MNPI claim tempting, but Standard II(A) turns on the source and nature of the information used, which here is public and non-confidential, not on whether a conclusion happens to precede a public announcement.'),
  ('ET09', 'Ethics', 'Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'Tipping', 'A corporate finance associate learns, through his work on a confidential pending acquisition, that his firm''s client will be acquired next week at a large premium to its current share price. He does not trade on this information himself, but tells his brother about the pending deal so his brother can buy shares before the announcement. The associate has:', '["Not violated Standard II(A), because he did not personally trade on the information","Violated only Standard IV(A) Loyalty, because sharing confidential deal information with a family member is primarily a breach of duty to his employer","Violated Standard II(A), because the Standard prohibits causing others to trade on material nonpublic information, not just trading on it personally"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard II(A) prohibits members from trading on material nonpublic information or causing others to trade on it, so tipping a family member to trade ahead of a material announcement is a direct violation even though the associate placed no order himself. Believing that only personal trading counts is the most common misconception about this Standard; the causing-others-to-trade language exists precisely to close that loophole. Standard IV(A) is a real Standard concerned with a member''s loyalty to his employer, and duties to the employer are indeed implicated by leaking confidential deal information, but tipping someone to trade ahead of a material announcement is squarely a Standard II(A) violation, not merely a loyalty issue.'),
  ('ET10', 'Ethics', 'Standard II(A) Material Nonpublic Information', 'Source of confidential information', 'A member overhears a public company''s chief financial officer privately confirm to another executive, in a non-public setting, that the company will miss its earnings guidance by a wide margin next week. The member has no other information suggesting this. Trading on this overheard information would violate:', '["Standard II(A), because the information is both material, since it is likely to affect the share price, and nonpublic, since it was confirmed privately by an insider and not yet disclosed","No Standard, because information obtained by overhearing a conversation, rather than through a business relationship, is not covered by Standard II(A)","Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts, because the member should have disclosed to the CFO that she overheard the conversation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Material nonpublic information is covered by Standard II(A) regardless of how it was obtained, whether overheard, leaked, or directly shared; the test is only whether the information is material and nonpublic, both true here given a wide earnings miss confirmed by the CFO himself. Carving out an exception for information obtained by overhearing rather than through a business relationship is tempting but invents a distinction the Standard does not make, since the source or channel of receipt does not matter. Standard VI(A) is a real Standard governing disclosure of a member''s own conflicts of interest, but it has no bearing on this fact pattern, which involves an MNPI trading question rather than any conflict of interest.'),
  ('ET11', 'Ethics', 'Standard II(B) Market Manipulation', 'Information-based manipulation', 'A trader spreads a false rumor across social media that a small, thinly traded company is about to be acquired at a large premium, intending to drive up the share price before selling his existing position at a profit. This conduct violates:', '["Standard II(A), because the rumor concerns material nonpublic information about an acquisition","Standard II(B), because spreading false information intended to affect a security''s price is information-based market manipulation","Standard I(C) Misrepresentation only, since the trader is not a supervised person and Standard II(B) applies only to registered broker-dealers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard II(B) prohibits both information-based manipulation, meaning disseminating false or misleading information, and transaction-based manipulation intended to distort prices or trading volume; fabricating an acquisition rumor to move the price is the textbook example of the former. Citing Standard II(A) is tempting because the rumor concerns an acquisition, but II(A) concerns trading on genuine confidential information, and this rumor is fabricated rather than real inside information, so there is no material nonpublic information to trade on at all. The claim that II(B) applies only to registered broker-dealers is also tempting since market manipulation sounds like a securities-regulation concept, but the Code and Standards apply to all CFA Institute members and candidates regardless of registration status.'),
  ('ET12', 'Ethics', 'Standard II(B) Market Manipulation', 'Transaction-based manipulation', 'Two traders who are friends, but who trade through unrelated accounts at different firms, agree in advance to repeatedly buy and sell the same illiquid stock back and forth between their two accounts at successively higher prices, with no real change in beneficial ownership intended, in order to attract other investors'' attention to the stock''s rising price and volume. This is:', '["Not a violation, because both parties voluntarily agreed to the trades and no false statement was made to anyone","A violation of Standard III(B) Fair Dealing, because the two traders are not treating other market participants fairly","A violation of Standard II(B), because creating artificial price or volume activity through pre-arranged trades is transaction-based market manipulation, regardless of whether any false statement was made"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard II(B) covers transaction-based manipulation, meaning trades designed to distort prices or create a misleading appearance of trading activity, even without any false statement being made, and this pre-arranged wash-trading pattern is precisely that. The absence of any false statement and the parties'' mutual consent make the no-violation conclusion tempting, but II(B) does not require a false statement; artificially manufactured price or volume activity is itself the violation. Standard III(B) is a real Standard, but it governs how a member treats its own clients when disseminating recommendations or allocating trades, not artificial trading designed to mislead the broader market.'),
  ('ET13', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, and Care', 'Client brokerage and soft dollars', 'A portfolio manager directs client trades to a particular broker that charges higher-than-average commissions, in exchange for that broker providing the manager''s firm with a general-purpose office productivity subscription unrelated to investment research. The extra commission cost is borne entirely by client accounts. This arrangement:', '["Violates Standard III(A), because client brokerage was used to obtain a benefit that does not help the manager serve the very clients whose commissions paid for it","Complies with Standard III(A), because any product or service obtained through client brokerage is permitted as long as it benefits the manager''s firm","Violates Standard III(C) Suitability, because paying higher commissions is never suitable for a client''s account"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard III(A) requires that client brokerage be used to benefit the clients who generated it, most commonly by paying for research or execution-related services that help the manager serve those clients; directing client commissions to pay for a general office productivity tool benefits the firm itself rather than the clients whose money paid for it, which breaches the duty of loyalty and care owed to those clients. The claim that any firm benefit is acceptable is tempting because soft-dollar arrangements are a normal and permitted part of the industry, but the arrangement must still benefit clients, not merely the manager''s own overhead. Standard III(C) is a real Standard, but it concerns whether a specific investment fits a client''s objectives and constraints, not how brokerage commissions are spent once generated.'),
  ('ET14', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, and Care', 'Client''s best interest', 'Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, and Care requires that, when managing a client''s portfolio, a member place:', '["The interests of the member''s employer above those of the client, since the employer pays the member''s compensation","The client''s interests above the interests of the member''s employer and above the member''s own personal interests","The interests of the investment industry''s overall reputation above the specific interests of any single client"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard III(A) requires members to act for the benefit of their clients and to place client interests before their employer''s interests and before their own personal interests. Elevating the employer''s interests is tempting because the member''s paycheck comes from the employer, but Standard III(A) exists precisely to prevent that everyday reality from overriding the client''s interests. Protecting the industry''s broader reputation is a real concern reflected elsewhere in the Code and Standards, but Standard III(A) is specifically about the individual client relationship, not the profession''s collective image.'),
  ('ET15', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(B) Fair Dealing', 'Disseminating recommendation changes', 'An analyst at Thistledown Capital upgrades her rating on a widely held stock. Before releasing the change to all subscribers simultaneously, she calls her firm''s largest institutional client to give that client a few hours'' advance notice, reasoning that the client''s size and importance to the firm''s revenue justifies the early call. This is:', '["Acceptable, because Standard III(B) only requires fair dealing among clients who pay identical fee rates","A violation of Standard VI(C) Referral Fees, because the firm is effectively rewarding the large client for its business relationship","A violation of Standard III(B), because Standard III(B) requires that investment recommendations and changes be disseminated to clients in a manner that treats all clients fairly, not based on the size of the account or revenue generated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard III(B) requires members to deal fairly and objectively with all clients when disseminating investment recommendations or changes to them, which precludes giving preferential early access to favored clients based on account size or revenue importance. Limiting fair dealing to clients on identical fee schedules is tempting because fee arrangements do vary by client, but Standard III(B) is not conditioned on fee structure; it requires fairness in the timing and manner of disseminating recommendations regardless of what a client pays. Standard VI(C) is a real Standard, but it concerns compensation received or paid for client referrals, which is not what is happening here at all.'),
  ('ET16', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(B) Fair Dealing', 'Trade allocation', 'A portfolio manager places a single block order to buy shares for twelve client accounts with similar mandates, but the order is only partially filled at the desired price. The manager allocates the entire available quantity to the three largest accounts, which also happen to pay the firm''s highest fees, leaving the other nine accounts with none of the shares. Absent any client-specific reason such as differing cash availability or investment restrictions, this allocation:', '["Violates Standard III(B), because partially filled orders must be allocated among similarly situated clients on a fair basis, such as pro rata, rather than favoring the accounts that pay the most in fees","Complies with Standard III(B), because a manager may always favor the clients who generate the most revenue for the firm","Violates Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements, because the manager is receiving extra compensation from the favored accounts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard III(B) requires fair treatment of clients when allocating trades, which for a partially filled block order generally means a pre-established, fair method such as pro-rata allocation among similarly situated accounts, not favoring accounts simply because they pay higher fees. Favoring the highest revenue-generating accounts is tempting because it sounds like ordinary business prioritization, but Standard III(B) exists specifically to prevent fee size or client importance from determining who receives scarce, favorable fills. Standard IV(B) is a real Standard, but it concerns a member''s outside compensation arrangements tied to services to clients, not the mechanics of allocating a single block trade among existing client accounts.'),
  ('ET17', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(C) Suitability', 'Updating the investment policy statement', 'A financial advisor''s client has, over several years, disclosed a significantly reduced risk tolerance following retirement and a large reduction in income. The advisor has not updated the client''s investment policy statement to reflect this change, and continues recommending the same growth-oriented equity strategy the client held while working. This is most likely a violation of:', '["Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts, because the advisor has an undisclosed incentive to keep the client in the same strategy","Standard III(C) Suitability, because a member must update a client''s investment policy statement and investment objectives regularly, and must consider a client''s changed circumstances before continuing to recommend investments","Standard V(B) Communication with Clients, because the advisor failed to explain the growth strategy''s risks clearly enough when it was first recommended"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard III(C) requires a member to regularly update a client''s investment policy statement and to consider the client''s changed financial circumstances, risk tolerance, and objectives before making or continuing investment recommendations; continuing a growth-oriented strategy after a major, disclosed reduction in risk tolerance and income without reassessing suitability is a direct violation. Standard VI(A) is a real Standard, but nothing in the facts suggests the advisor has an undisclosed personal or financial conflict driving the recommendation, only a failure to update the suitability analysis itself. Standard V(B) is also real, but it concerns how clearly a recommendation''s basis and risks are communicated, not the separate duty to keep the suitability assessment current as a client''s circumstances evolve.'),
  ('ET18', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(C) Suitability', 'Portfolio-level suitability', 'A client''s written investment policy statement calls for a diversified portfolio with no more than 5% in any single speculative holding. An advisor recommends a highly speculative small-cap stock, representing 3% of the client''s total portfolio value, as one part of a broader diversified allocation consistent with the rest of the client''s investment policy statement. Evaluated in isolation, the stock would be far too risky for this client. Under Standard III(C), the recommendation is best evaluated by considering:', '["Only the specific holding''s own risk characteristics in isolation, since Standard III(C) evaluates the suitability of each security independently of the rest of the portfolio","Only whether the client verbally agreed to accept the risk of this specific stock at the time of the phone call recommending it","How the holding fits within the context of the client''s total portfolio and investment policy statement, since a specific security''s suitability is assessed in light of the portfolio it becomes part of"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard III(C) requires suitability to be assessed in the context of the client''s total portfolio and investment policy statement, not by judging a single security in isolation; a speculative holding that would be inappropriate as an entire portfolio can still be suitable as a small, bounded piece of a diversified whole, which is the case here since the position falls within the client''s own stated limit. Evaluating the stock in isolation is a tempting simplification, since the position genuinely looks too risky standalone, but Standard III(C) is explicit that portfolio context governs. Relying only on verbal agreement in the moment is also tempting because client consent feels reassuring, but Standard III(C) requires an actual suitability analysis grounded in the investment policy statement, not merely securing the client''s verbal sign-off.'),
  ('ET19', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(D) Performance Presentation', 'Cherry-picking results', 'An asset manager oversees fifteen similarly mandated institutional accounts with a wide range of returns over the past three years. When marketing to a new prospective client, the manager presents only the single best-performing account''s results as representative of the strategy''s overall performance. This is:', '["A violation of Standard III(D), because presenting a hand-picked, unrepresentative account misrepresents the strategy''s actual performance to prospective clients","Acceptable, because Standard III(D) only restricts performance claims made to existing clients, not to prospective clients","A violation of Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity, because the manager is not being objective about the strategy''s true risk level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard III(D) requires that performance information presented to clients and prospective clients be fair, accurate, and complete, which prohibits selecting only the best-performing account out of a larger, more representative group to imply that result reflects the strategy generally. Restricting the Standard to existing clients only is tempting because much of the Code and Standards focuses on the existing client relationship, but Standard III(D) explicitly extends to prospective clients as well, since misleading marketing materials cause harm before any account is even opened. Standard I(B) is a real Standard, but it concerns a member''s independence and objectivity in analysis and recommendations, not the separate issue of selectively presenting historical performance data.'),
  ('ET20', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(D) Performance Presentation', 'Survivorship bias in composites', 'An asset manager maintains a composite of forty similarly managed accounts for performance reporting purposes. Over the past five years, twelve accounts in this strategy underperformed badly and were closed by their clients; the manager excludes those twelve terminated accounts from the composite entirely, reporting results based only on the twenty-eight accounts that remained open and outperformed. The manager tells prospective clients the composite includes all accounts ever managed in this strategy. This presentation:', '["Violates only Standard I(C) Misrepresentation, because the false statement about including all accounts is a misrepresentation of fact unrelated to performance presentation rules","Violates Standard III(D), because omitting terminated, underperforming accounts from a composite inflates the reported track record and misrepresents the strategy''s true historical performance, compounding a separate Standard I(C) misrepresentation in the same episode","Does not violate any Standard, because a manager is only required to report on currently open accounts, and closed accounts are no longer part of the manager''s current business"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard III(D) requires performance presentations to be fair, accurate, and complete; systematically dropping terminated, underperforming accounts from a composite is a classic survivorship-bias distortion that inflates the reported track record, and the false claim that the composite includes all accounts ever managed is a separate, additional Standard I(C) misrepresentation layered on top of the same episode. Treating this as only a Standard I(C) issue understates the harm, since the exclusion of the accounts itself, regardless of the false verbal claim, already distorts the performance record under Standard III(D). Believing that closed accounts can simply be dropped because they are no longer part of current business is tempting, since reporting on ended relationships can feel irrelevant, but doing so here creates a materially misleading track record, which is exactly what Standard III(D) is designed to prevent.'),
  ('ET21', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality', 'Duty of confidentiality', 'A member learns confidential details about a client''s family situation and financial goals while preparing a financial plan. Absent any legal requirement to disclose the information or the client''s own consent, Standard III(E) requires the member to:', '["Share the information freely with colleagues at the same firm, since internal sharing does not count as disclosure to an outside party","Disclose the information only if a family member of the client specifically requests it","Keep the information confidential, since it was gathered in connection with the professional client relationship"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard III(E) requires members to keep confidential information learned in the course of a professional relationship confidential, unless disclosure is required by law, the client consents, or the information concerns illegal activity by the client. Assuming internal sharing is automatically fine is tempting because colleagues within the same firm are not literally an outside party, but Standard III(E) still requires internal access to be limited to those with a legitimate need to know in serving the client, not shared freely. A family member''s request is also tempting to treat as an exception, but a relative asking is not the client''s own consent and is not one of the Standard''s recognized exceptions.'),
  ('ET22', 'Ethics', 'Standard III(E) Preservation of Confidentiality', 'Exception for illegal client activity', 'While reviewing a client''s account activity, an advisor discovers strong evidence that the client is using the account to launder proceeds from an illegal enterprise. Under Standard III(E), the advisor''s duty of client confidentiality:', '["May yield in this situation, because Standard III(E) recognizes an exception when the information concerns illegal activities by the client","Cannot yield under any circumstances, because Standard III(E) treats client confidentiality as absolute regardless of what the client has done","Only yields if the client provides written consent to disclose the suspected laundering activity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard III(E) recognizes exceptions to client confidentiality, including when the information concerns illegal activities conducted by the client, in addition to situations where disclosure is required by law or the client consents. Treating confidentiality as absolute is tempting because the duty of confidentiality is described as a serious, general obligation, but the Standard itself carves out this illegal-activity exception rather than making confidentiality unconditional. Requiring the client''s own consent before disclosing evidence of the client''s own wrongdoing misunderstands the exception, since the whole point of the illegal-activity exception is that it can apply without needing the wrongdoing client''s cooperation.'),
  ('ET23', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(A) Loyalty', 'Competing with employer', 'While still employed at her current firm and prior to giving any notice of resignation, a portfolio manager begins contacting several of her firm''s largest clients, urging them to move their accounts to the new firm she plans to join next month. This conduct:', '["Complies with Standard IV(A), because a member is always free to plan for future employment while still working for a current employer","Violates Standard IV(A) Loyalty, because soliciting an employer''s clients to leave before the member has actually departed the firm undermines the employer''s business interests while still employed","Violates Standard II(B) Market Manipulation, because moving client accounts between firms affects trading volume at both firms"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard IV(A) requires members to act for their employer''s benefit and not undermine the employer''s legitimate business interests while still employed, and actively soliciting the employer''s own clients to depart before the member has left crosses from permissible preparation into disloyal competition. Treating all preparation for future employment as automatically acceptable is tempting since some preparatory steps are indeed permitted, but Standard IV(A) draws a line at actively soliciting the current employer''s clients while still on its payroll. Standard II(B) is a real Standard, but it concerns manipulating security prices or trading activity, which has nothing to do with clients moving their accounts between advisory firms.'),
  ('ET24', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(A) Loyalty', 'Permissible job-search activity', 'While still employed, an analyst updates his resume on his own time, interviews with a competing firm after work hours, and privately negotiates his future compensation, all without using his current employer''s resources, systems, or confidential information, and without soliciting any of his current employer''s clients. Once he accepts an offer, he gives the standard notice required by his contract before departing. Under Standard IV(A), his job-search conduct before resigning was:', '["A violation, because any contact with a competing employer while still employed automatically breaches the duty of loyalty","A violation, because Standard IV(A) requires an employee to disclose any job search to the current employer as soon as it begins","Not a violation, because ordinary job-seeking activity conducted on personal time, without using the employer''s resources, confidential information, or soliciting its clients, does not by itself breach the duty of loyalty"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard IV(A) prohibits undermining an employer''s business interests, such as soliciting its clients or misusing its confidential resources, but it does not prohibit ordinary job-seeking activity conducted on personal time without those elements, since employees are generally permitted to seek new employment before resigning. Treating any contact with a competitor as automatically disloyal is tempting because it sounds like the analyst is working against his current employer, but the duty of loyalty targets active harm to the employer''s interests, not the mere fact of looking elsewhere. Requiring advance disclosure of a job search is also tempting since it sounds like transparency would be the safest course, but Standard IV(A) imposes no such affirmative disclosure obligation on an employee who is simply exploring other opportunities properly.'),
  ('ET25', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(A) Loyalty', 'Employer records upon departure', 'Before resigning, an analyst copies her firm''s proprietary client contact list and several internally developed valuation models onto a personal drive, intending to use them at her new firm. She does not copy any client account numbers or confidential account holdings. Separately, she also retains the general industry knowledge and modeling skills she developed over her career, which she plans to keep using at her new job. Under Standard IV(A), which of these is permissible after she leaves?', '["Only the general industry knowledge and skills she developed are hers to keep and use; the proprietary client list and internally developed models remain the firm''s property and may not be taken","Both the client list and the internally developed models may be taken, because she personally worked on developing them while employed","Neither may be retained, including her own general skills and knowledge, because all professional development that occurred during her employment belongs to the firm"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard IV(A) permits a departing member to keep the general skills, knowledge, and experience gained during employment, since those are the member''s own, but it does not permit taking the employer''s proprietary records, such as client contact lists and firm-developed models, which remain the employer''s property regardless of the member''s personal role in creating or using them. Believing that personal involvement in developing the models or list justifies taking them is tempting, since the analyst did put in real work on them, but Standard IV(A) protects the employer''s ownership of firm records created for and on behalf of the firm. Treating even general skills and knowledge as the employer''s property goes too far in the other direction, since Standard IV(A) explicitly allows a departing member to use the expertise built over a career, which is not the same as the firm''s proprietary records.'),
  ('ET26', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements', 'Undisclosed outside compensation', 'An advisor refers several clients to an estate-planning attorney and, without informing her employer, personally receives a cash referral payment from the attorney for each client referred. This is:', '["Permitted, because the payments come from the attorney rather than from the advisor''s own clients","A violation of Standard IV(B), because a member must obtain written consent from the employer before accepting compensation from a third party that relates to the member''s employment services","A violation of Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions, because the advisor is prioritizing the attorney''s business interests over the clients'' interests"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard IV(B) requires members to obtain written consent from their employer before accepting compensation, gifts, or other benefits from third parties that are in addition to compensation from the employer and that relate to the member''s employment services, such as referral payments tied to the advisor''s own client base. The source of the payment being the attorney rather than the clients is tempting because no client money changed hands directly, but Standard IV(B) is triggered by any outside compensation connected to the member''s employment activities, regardless of who technically pays it. Standard VI(B) is a real Standard, but it concerns the timing priority of a member''s personal securities transactions relative to client and employer transactions, not undisclosed third-party compensation for referrals.'),
  ('ET27', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements', 'Employer consent requirement', 'Standard IV(B) Additional Compensation Arrangements requires that a member who wishes to accept compensation or other benefits from a party other than the employer, in exchange for services that might create a conflict with the employer''s interests, must first:', '["Wait until after receiving the benefit to mention it to the employer if the amount involved is small","Obtain a verbal acknowledgment from a coworker who is aware of the arrangement","Obtain written consent from the employer, disclosing the nature, amount, and duration of the arrangement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard IV(B) requires written consent from the employer before accepting such additional compensation, with disclosure of the arrangement''s nature, the compensation''s approximate amount, and its duration. Waiting until afterward, and treating small amounts as exempt, is tempting because immateriality feels low-risk, but the Standard requires prior written consent regardless of the size of the benefit. A coworker''s informal verbal acknowledgment is also tempting since it sounds like transparency within the firm, but Standard IV(B) specifically requires the employer''s own written consent, not merely another employee''s awareness of the arrangement.'),
  ('ET28', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors', 'Adequate compliance procedures', 'Under Standard IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors, a member with supervisory authority over other employees satisfies her duty by:', '["Making reasonable efforts to detect and prevent violations of applicable laws, rules, regulations, and the Code and Standards by establishing and enforcing adequate compliance procedures","Personally reviewing every trade and communication made by each subordinate before it occurs, since only complete, individual pre-clearance satisfies the Standard","Delegating all compliance responsibility to the firm''s legal department, since supervisory duty under the Standard belongs to lawyers rather than investment personnel"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard IV(C) requires supervisors to make reasonable efforts to detect and prevent violations by establishing, maintaining, and enforcing adequate compliance procedures, rather than guaranteeing that no violation will ever occur. Requiring pre-clearance of literally every trade and communication is tempting because it sounds maximally thorough, but the Standard''s own language is one of reasonableness, not an unattainable standard of perfect, individualized surveillance of every act. Delegating all responsibility to the legal department is also tempting because compliance sounds like a legal function, but Standard IV(C) places the supervisory responsibility on the member who holds actual supervisory authority over the employees in question, not automatically on legal counsel.'),
  ('ET29', 'Ethics', 'Standard IV(C) Responsibilities of Supervisors', 'Failing to respond to red flags', 'A compliance report flags that one trader on a supervisor''s team has, for several consecutive months, generated unusually high commissions relative to the rest of the team, a pattern commonly associated with excessive trading. The supervisor notices the report but takes no action to investigate, and the trader continues the same pattern for another year before regulators eventually uncover client-harming churning. Under Standard IV(C), the supervisor''s failure to investigate the initial warning signs is:', '["Not a violation, because Standard IV(C) only applies once a violation has been conclusively proven, not while it is merely suspected","A violation of Standard IV(C), because a supervisor who becomes aware of indications of possible misconduct, such as an unusual commission pattern, must investigate and respond, not ignore the warning signs","A violation attributable solely to the trader under Standard III(A) Loyalty, Prudence, and Care, with no separate responsibility falling on the supervisor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard IV(C) requires a supervisor who becomes aware of indications that a violation might be occurring, such as an unexplained pattern of unusually high commissions, to investigate promptly and thoroughly and to take steps to prevent further misconduct, rather than waiting for definitive proof before acting. Requiring conclusive proof before any duty attaches is tempting because it avoids acting on mere suspicion, but Standard IV(C) is explicitly triggered by reasonable indications of possible wrongdoing, not only by confirmed violations. Attributing the failure solely to the trader is also tempting since the trader is the one directly harming clients, but Standard IV(C) imposes an independent responsibility on the supervisor personally for failing to investigate and respond to the warning signs under her own watch.'),
  ('ET30', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Relying on issuer-provided materials', 'An analyst issues a buy recommendation on a company''s stock, basing her entire analysis on the company''s own investor-relations presentation and press releases, without independently verifying any of the company''s key operating or financial claims or reviewing independent third-party data. Under Standard V(A), this research process:', '["Fully satisfies the Standard, because a company''s own investor-relations materials are an authoritative and sufficient source for any research report about that company","Fully satisfies the Standard, so long as the analyst discloses in the report that the analysis relies entirely on company-provided materials","Fails to satisfy the Standard, because Standard V(A) requires a member to have a reasonable and adequate basis for a recommendation, supported by appropriate independent research and analysis rather than reliance on a single potentially self-interested source"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard V(A) requires members to exercise diligence and have a reasonable and adequate basis, supported by appropriate research, for any recommendation or action, which generally requires independent verification rather than relying solely on materials produced by the company being recommended, since issuer-provided materials have an inherent incentive to present the company favorably. Treating company materials as automatically authoritative is tempting because the company should know its own business best, but that same closeness to the subject is exactly why independent verification matters under Standard V(A). Disclosure of the sourcing limitation is also tempting because transparency is valued elsewhere in the Code and Standards, but disclosing a shortcut does not cure the underlying failure to actually perform adequate independent diligence that Standard V(A) requires.'),
  ('ET31', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis', 'Understanding a quantitative model', 'Standard V(A) Diligence and Reasonable Basis requires that a member who uses a third-party quantitative model to generate investment recommendations:', '["Develop a sufficient understanding of the model''s methodology and key assumptions, so the resulting recommendations rest on a reasonable and adequate basis rather than blind reliance on an unexamined output","Disclose the model''s existence to clients, after which no further understanding of its inner workings is required under the Standard","Avoid quantitative models entirely, since Standard V(A) requires that all recommendations be based solely on fundamental, non-model-based analysis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard V(A) requires a reasonable and adequate basis for any recommendation, which, when a model is involved, means the member must sufficiently understand the model''s methodology, inputs, and limitations rather than blindly trusting its output. Believing disclosure alone is enough is tempting because transparency is valued throughout the Code and Standards, but merely telling clients a model was used does not substitute for the member actually understanding whether that model''s basis is sound. Banning quantitative models outright is also tempting since it sounds like a safe, conservative reading, but Standard V(A) does not prefer one analytical method over another; it requires diligence in understanding whatever method is actually used.'),
  ('ET32', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(B) Communication with Clients and Prospective Clients', 'Distinguishing fact from opinion', 'Standard V(B) Communication with Clients and Prospective Clients requires that, in preparing research reports and recommendations, a member must:', '["Avoid stating any opinions at all, limiting reports strictly to verified historical facts","Clearly distinguish between statements of fact and statements that reflect the member''s own opinions or projections","Include every opinion the member holds about a company, regardless of whether it is relevant to the specific recommendation being made"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard V(B) requires members to clearly separate opinions from facts when communicating with clients, so that clients can judge for themselves how much weight to give a forward-looking view versus a verified fact. Prohibiting any opinion at all is tempting because it sounds like the safest way to avoid misleading anyone, but investment recommendations are inherently forward-looking judgments, and Standard V(B) requires labeling opinions clearly rather than eliminating them. Requiring every personal opinion to be disclosed regardless of relevance is also tempting since it sounds maximally transparent, but the Standard calls for clarity about what is fact versus opinion in the material actually presented, not an unbounded dump of every view the member happens to hold.'),
  ('ET33', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(B) Communication with Clients and Prospective Clients', 'Disclosing process limitations', 'An advisory firm builds client portfolios using a proprietary algorithm that automatically screens out entire industry sectors based on criteria the firm does not explain to clients or make available on request. Clients are told only that portfolios are built using a sophisticated, proprietary process. Under Standard V(B), this level of disclosure:', '["Is adequate, because proprietary methods are always exempt from any disclosure requirement in order to protect the firm''s trade secrets","Is adequate, because Standard V(B) requires disclosure of investment recommendations themselves, not the process used to arrive at them","Is likely inadequate, because Standard V(B) requires a member to disclose to clients the basic format and general principles of the investment process used, even when specific proprietary details can reasonably be withheld"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard V(B) requires members to disclose the basic format and general principles of the investment processes used to analyze investments, select securities, and construct portfolios, so that clients understand in general terms how decisions are made, even though granular proprietary details, such as an exact formula, can reasonably remain confidential. Treating proprietary status as a full exemption is tempting because firms do have a legitimate interest in protecting trade secrets, but Standard V(B) only protects the specific proprietary details, not the basic explanation of the process''s general approach and limitations. Limiting the Standard to the recommendations themselves is also tempting since the recommendation is the client-facing output, but Standard V(B) explicitly extends to explaining the process behind the recommendation, not merely announcing its conclusion.'),
  ('ET34', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention', 'Supporting documentation for recommendations', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention requires that a member who issues an investment recommendation:', '["Retain records supporting the basis for the recommendation and the analysis behind it, so the reasoning can be demonstrated if the recommendation is later questioned","Retain records only for as long as the specific client who received the recommendation remains with the firm","Retain records only if the recommendation results in an actual securities transaction being executed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard V(C) requires members to maintain records that support the reasoning and analysis behind their recommendations and actions, so that the basis for a judgment can later be reconstructed and demonstrated if questioned by a regulator, employer, or client. Tying the retention period to a specific client''s tenure with the firm is tempting because it sounds like a natural boundary, but the Standard''s retention obligation is not defined by whether a particular client relationship continues. Limiting the duty to recommendations that actually result in a completed transaction is also tempting since executed trades feel like the consequential events, but Standard V(C) applies to the analysis and basis for a recommendation itself, regardless of whether the client ultimately acted on it.'),
  ('ET35', 'Ethics', 'Standard V(C) Record Retention', 'Ownership of research records upon departure', 'An analyst leaves her firm to join a competitor. Before departing, she keeps a personal copy of detailed research files she authored while employed, including data sources, models, and notes supporting recommendations she issued, reasoning that since Standard V(C) required her personally to retain these records, they must belong to her rather than to the firm. Under Standard V(C) together with Standard IV(A) Loyalty, this reasoning is:', '["Correct, because Standard V(C) imposes the record-retention duty on the individual analyst, so the records are the analyst''s personal property to keep","Incorrect, because Standard V(C)''s record-retention duty runs to the member''s obligation to be able to substantiate recommendations, but the records themselves are generally the firm''s property created within the scope of employment, so taking them without the firm''s permission also implicates Standard IV(A)","Incorrect, because Standard V(C) does not apply to individual analysts at all, only to the firm''s compliance department as a whole"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard V(C) requires that adequate records supporting investment analysis be maintained and available, and while a member has a professional obligation connected to that recordkeeping, the records themselves, created using firm resources within the scope of employment, are generally the firm''s property; taking them without authorization upon departure separately implicates the duty of loyalty under Standard IV(A), which protects the employer''s proprietary records. Concluding that a retention duty implies personal ownership is a tempting but backwards inference, since the obligation to maintain records for accountability purposes does not transfer title in those records to the individual who happened to create them on the job. Standard V(C) does apply to individual members with recommendation responsibilities, not only to a firm''s compliance function as a collective matter, so the claim that it is inapplicable to her personally is also incorrect.'),
  ('ET36', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts', 'Board seat conflicts', 'A portfolio manager sits on the board of directors of a publicly traded company. Her firm''s investment team is considering recommending that company''s stock to clients for the first time. Under Standard VI(A), before any recommendation is made, the manager must:', '["Resign from the board immediately, since Standard VI(A) prohibits members from simultaneously holding a board seat and recommending that company''s securities under any circumstances","Do nothing further, since board membership is a public fact that clients could discover on their own through a routine search","Disclose her board position to her employer and to clients, so that the potential conflict between her board duties and her role in the investment recommendation is transparent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard VI(A) requires members to disclose to their employer, clients, and prospective clients all matters that could reasonably be expected to impair their independence or objectivity, including board service at a company under consideration for a recommendation, so the conflict is transparent to those relying on the recommendation. Assuming resignation is mandatory is tempting because it removes the conflict entirely, but Standard VI(A) requires disclosure of conflicts, not automatic elimination of every dual role; a board seat can coexist with a recommendation once properly disclosed and managed. Assuming that public availability of the fact satisfies the Standard is also tempting, since board membership is typically a matter of public record, but Standard VI(A) requires the member to affirmatively disclose the conflict, not rely on clients to independently discover it themselves.'),
  ('ET37', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(A) Disclosure of Conflicts', 'Personal share ownership', 'A member owns a significant personal stake in a small, thinly traded company. Before recommending that company''s stock to clients, Standard VI(A) requires the member to:', '["Disclose the ownership stake to her employer and to clients so they can weigh the potential conflict when evaluating the recommendation","Sell the entire position immediately, since Standard VI(A) prohibits members from ever personally owning a security they also recommend","Say nothing, since Standard VI(A) only requires disclosure of compensation received from third parties, not disclosure of the member''s own personal investment holdings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard VI(A) requires disclosure of all matters, including beneficial ownership of securities, that could reasonably be expected to impair a member''s objectivity, and a significant personal stake in a company being recommended is exactly this kind of conflict, which must be disclosed to the employer and to clients rather than concealed. Requiring the member to sell the position entirely is tempting because it seems like it would remove the conflict outright, but Standard VI(A)''s core requirement is disclosure, not a categorical prohibition on personal ownership of recommended securities. Limiting the disclosure duty to third-party compensation only is also tempting because that is one common example the Standard covers, but Standard VI(A) reaches any material conflict, including the member''s own personal holdings, not compensation arrangements alone.'),
  ('ET38', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions', 'Front-running client trades', 'A portfolio manager decides to place a large buy order for a stock on behalf of several client accounts. Before entering the client order, he buys shares for his own personal account, anticipating that the client order''s size will push the stock''s price up shortly afterward. This is:', '["Not a violation, because the manager used his own money and did not use any client funds to purchase his personal shares","A violation of Standard VI(B), because client and employer transactions must be given priority over a member''s own personal transactions in the same security","A violation of Standard II(B) Market Manipulation, because the client order itself is what will move the market price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard VI(B) requires that transactions for clients and employers take priority over a member''s personal transactions, which specifically prohibits trading ahead of a client order the member knows is about to be entered in order to benefit personally from the anticipated price impact. Using only his own money is tempting because no client assets were misappropriated, but Standard VI(B) is not about whose money is used; it is about the sequencing priority between personal and client trades in the same security. Standard II(B) is a real Standard, but market manipulation concerns artificially distorting prices through deceptive conduct, whereas this client order is a legitimate transaction; the actual violation is the manager''s improper personal trade sequenced ahead of it, which is a priority-of-transactions issue, not manipulation.'),
  ('ET39', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions', 'Waiting period after recommendations', 'Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions is best described as requiring that, with respect to a member''s personal trading in a security that the member also recommends to clients:', '["Personal trades be prohibited entirely in any security the member ever recommends to any client, for as long as the member remains employed in the industry","Personal trades be permitted at any time, since a member''s compensation is not directly linked to the timing of any single client''s transaction","Client and employer transactions receive priority, so a member should not personally trade in a manner that disadvantages clients, such as trading ahead of client transactions or recommendations before clients have had an adequate opportunity to act on them"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard VI(B) requires that client and employer interests take priority over the member''s personal trading interests, meaning a member must avoid trading in a way that disadvantages clients, such as acting on a recommendation personally before clients have had a fair opportunity to receive and act on it themselves. A complete, permanent ban on ever personally owning a recommended security is tempting because it sounds like the most cautious possible rule, but Standard VI(B) is about relative priority and timing, not an absolute prohibition on personal ownership of recommended securities. Assuming unrestricted personal trading is fine since compensation is not directly tied to a single trade is also tempting, but Standard VI(B) protects against the timing advantage a member could exploit regardless of how compensation happens to be structured.'),
  ('ET40', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(C) Referral Fees', 'Disclosing fees received for referrals', 'An investment advisor regularly refers clients who need tax preparation services to a particular accounting firm, which pays the advisor a percentage of the fees it collects from each referred client. Under Standard VI(C), the advisor must:', '["Disclose the referral arrangement, including its nature and the associated compensation, to clients before they are referred, so clients can evaluate the advisor''s objectivity in making the referral","Do nothing further, provided the accounting firm itself independently discloses the arrangement to clients at the time services begin","Decline all compensation for the referral, since Standard VI(C) permits referral arrangements only when they are made entirely free of charge to the referring party"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard VI(C) requires members to disclose to clients any compensation or benefit received for referring them to other service providers, including the nature and, where possible, the value of the benefit, so clients can judge whether the referral is influenced by the advisor''s own financial interest. Relying on the accounting firm to make the disclosure instead is tempting because the compensation flows from that firm, but Standard VI(C) places the disclosure duty on the referring member, not on the third party receiving the referral. Believing referral fees are banned outright is also tempting since accepting payment for a referral sounds like it could bias the advisor''s judgment, but Standard VI(C) permits referral fee arrangements as long as they are properly disclosed, rather than prohibiting them altogether.'),
  ('ET41', 'Ethics', 'Standard VI(C) Referral Fees', 'Disclosing fees paid for referrals', 'An advisory firm pays a flat fee to an independent insurance agent for every new client the agent refers to the firm. Under Standard VI(C), the firm''s members must:', '["Keep the payment arrangement confidential, since Standard VI(C) only requires disclosure of fees a member personally receives, not fees the member''s firm pays out to others","Disclose to prospective clients referred by the agent that the firm pays the agent for the referral, so clients understand the agent''s incentive in directing them to the firm","Take no action, since only payments between two members of CFA Institute trigger any disclosure obligation under Standard VI(C)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard VI(C) requires disclosure of both referral compensation a member receives and compensation the member''s firm pays to others for client referrals, so that a referred client understands the referring party had a financial incentive to make the referral. Limiting disclosure to compensation personally received is tempting because it covers the more commonly discussed half of the rule, but Standard VI(C) explicitly also covers fees paid out for referrals into the firm. Requiring that both parties be CFA Institute members is also tempting since the Code and Standards govern members'' conduct, but the disclosure obligation applies to the member''s own referral payment practices regardless of whether the outside party receiving payment happens to hold the CFA credential.'),
  ('ET42', 'Ethics', 'Standard VII(A) Conduct as Members and Candidates in the CFA Program', 'Exam confidentiality', 'The day after sitting for a CFA exam, a candidate posts on a public online forum describing, in specific detail, several questions she remembers from the exam, in order to help future candidates understand the exam''s style. Under Standard VII(A), this conduct:', '["Is acceptable, because sharing exam content after the exam has ended cannot compromise the security of an exam that has already been administered","Is acceptable, because candidates own the right to discuss their own individual exam experience however they choose once they have completed testing","Violates Standard VII(A), because candidates must keep specific exam content confidential and may not disclose it, regardless of when the disclosure occurs relative to their own test date"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard VII(A) requires candidates to keep the specific content of CFA exam questions confidential at all times and prohibits disclosing that content to anyone, a rule that protects exam security for all candidates across testing windows, not only for the individual who has just finished testing. Believing that the exam being over removes any confidentiality concern is tempting, but the exam program reuses question content and administers exams across a broad testing window, so a completed personal exam session does not end the confidentiality obligation. Framing this as a personal ownership right over one''s own exam experience is also tempting, since the experience did happen to that candidate, but Standard VII(A) specifically restricts disclosing the content of exam questions, which is not the candidate''s own personal story to share freely.'),
  ('ET43', 'Ethics', 'Standard VII(A) Conduct as Members and Candidates in the CFA Program', 'Exam-day misconduct', 'During a scheduled break in a CFA exam, a candidate brings his personal mobile phone into the restroom, in violation of the exam administrator''s explicit rule that no personal electronic devices may be accessed during any part of the exam appointment, including breaks. He is later found to have used the break to look up an unrelated definition. Under Standard VII(A), this conduct:', '["Violates Standard VII(A), because candidates must comply with all CFA Institute testing policies and procedures, and accessing a prohibited device during the exam appointment breaches that duty regardless of what the candidate actually used it for","Does not violate Standard VII(A), because the candidate did not look up anything related to the actual exam content","Violates only the exam administrator''s contractual terms of service, which is a matter entirely separate from the CFA Institute Code and Standards"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Standard VII(A) requires candidates to comply with the rules and procedures governing the administration of the CFA exam, and accessing a prohibited personal device during the testing appointment breaches that duty regardless of what specific content was accessed, since the rule exists to preserve exam integrity and prevent even the appearance of improper access. Believing that looking up something unrelated to the exam excuses the conduct is tempting, since no exam content was compromised in this instance, but Standard VII(A) is violated by breaking the testing rules themselves, not only by cases where content is actually misused. Treating this as purely a contractual, non-ethical matter is also tempting because the rule was set by the exam administrator rather than stated directly in the Code, but Standard VII(A) explicitly incorporates compliance with CFA Institute''s rules and procedures for the exam into the candidate''s ethical obligations.'),
  ('ET44', 'Ethics', 'Standard VII(B) Reference to CFA Institute, the CFA Designation, and the CFA Program', 'Improper reference to the charter', 'A charterholder tells prospective clients that, because he holds the CFA charter, his personal stock picks are guaranteed to outperform the market over any five-year period. This statement is best characterized as a violation of:', '["Standard I(C) Misrepresentation only, since the guarantee of outperformance is simply a false statement of fact unrelated to his credential","Standard VII(B), because it misrepresents what holding the CFA charter itself means, improperly implying that the credential guarantees superior investment performance, which compounds a related Standard I(C) misrepresentation concern in the same statement","No Standard, because members are always free to describe their own personal investment track record however they choose when marketing to prospective clients"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard VII(B) specifically prohibits members from implying that the CFA charter itself guarantees superior investment performance or ability, and this statement ties an outperformance guarantee directly to holding the charter, which is exactly the kind of misuse of the designation VII(B) targets, alongside the more general Standard I(C) misrepresentation concern that any performance guarantee raises on its own. Attributing this only to Standard I(C) is tempting, since guaranteeing performance is indeed also a Standard I(C) issue in its own right, but framing the guarantee as flowing from the charter specifically brings Standard VII(B) into play as well, since that Standard exists precisely to prevent overstating what the credential itself means. Believing members have unlimited freedom to describe their own track record is also tempting, since a track record can generally be discussed, but no Standard permits a guarantee of future outperformance tied to holding the charter.'),
  ('ET45', 'Ethics', 'Standard VII(B) Reference to CFA Institute, the CFA Designation, and the CFA Program', 'Referencing candidacy correctly', 'A Level I candidate who has registered for the next scheduled exam wants to describe her status accurately on her professional profile. Under Standard VII(B), the appropriate way to reference her status is to state that she:', '["Is a partial CFA charterholder, having completed one of the three required exam levels","Has earned the CFA designation on a provisional basis pending completion of the remaining levels","Is a candidate in the CFA Program who is registered to sit for an upcoming Level I exam, without implying that any charter or partial designation has been earned"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard VII(B) permits candidates to accurately reference their enrollment and registration in the CFA Program, such as stating that they are registered to sit for a specific upcoming exam level, but prohibits implying that any charter, partial charter, or provisional designation has been earned before it actually has. Describing herself as a partial charterholder is tempting because completing one exam level is genuine, verifiable progress, but the charter is awarded only upon completing the entire program and its other requirements, not level by level. Describing the designation as held on a provisional basis is also tempting since it sounds like an honest acknowledgment of incomplete status, but Standard VII(B) does not recognize any such provisional or partial designation; candidacy and the charter are distinct statuses that may not be blended together.'),
  ('ET46', 'Ethics', 'GIPS', 'Purpose of GIPS', 'The Global Investment Performance Standards (GIPS) exist primarily to:', '["Enable fair, consistent, apples-to-apples comparison of investment performance across different firms and different time periods, and to prevent misleading performance claims","Guarantee a minimum rate of return to investors in any GIPS-compliant composite","Replace a firm''s external financial statement audit requirements with a single, unified performance standard"]'::jsonb, 0, 'GIPS exist to promote fair representation and full disclosure of investment performance results, enabling prospective clients to compare performance across firms and time periods on a consistent, apples-to-apples basis and reducing the risk of misleading performance claims. Believing GIPS guarantees a minimum return is tempting because the standards involve rigorous, formal performance calculation rules, but GIPS governs how performance is calculated and presented, not the actual investment outcomes a composite achieves. Believing GIPS replaces a financial statement audit is also tempting since both involve independent standards and rigor, but GIPS addresses performance presentation specifically and is entirely separate from a firm''s financial statement audit obligations.'),
  ('ET47', 'Ethics', 'GIPS', 'Firm-wide compliance', 'Under the fundamentals of GIPS compliance, a firm that wants to claim compliance with GIPS must:', '["Comply only with respect to the specific composite being marketed to a given prospective client at that time","Comply on a firm-wide basis, since a firm may not claim compliance for only a favorable selection of its composites while excluding others from the compliant process","Obtain a separate compliance certification for each individual client account it manages"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A core fundamental of GIPS compliance is that it applies on a firm-wide basis; a firm cannot claim GIPS compliance for only a hand-picked subset of composites, such as its best-performing ones, while leaving other composites outside the compliant process, since that would undermine the very comparability GIPS is meant to provide. Limiting compliance to whichever composite is being pitched to a specific prospective client at the moment is tempting because it mirrors how marketing conversations actually happen one client at a time, but GIPS compliance is a firm-wide claim, not a claim that can be selectively made composite by composite depending on the audience. Requiring a separate certification for each individual client account is also tempting since it sounds thorough, but GIPS compliance is assessed and claimed at the firm and composite level, not account by account.'),
  ('ET48', 'Ethics', 'GIPS', 'Defining the firm', 'An asset management company has three distinct business units: a retail mutual fund unit, an institutional separate account unit, and a private wealth unit, all operating under common ownership and using shared research and trading resources. The company wants to claim GIPS compliance only for its institutional separate account unit, defining that unit alone as the firm for GIPS purposes, in order to exclude its weaker-performing retail composites from the compliant process. Under GIPS fundamentals of compliance, this approach is:', '["Acceptable, because GIPS allows a company to define the firm however produces the most favorable composite results for marketing purposes","Acceptable, because GIPS compliance is always assessed at the individual composite level rather than at the level of any defined firm","Problematic, because the definition of the firm for GIPS purposes must be applied consistently and reflect the firm as it is actually held out to clients and the public, not redrawn narrowly to exclude unfavorable composites from firm-wide compliance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'GIPS requires a firm to define itself, for compliance purposes, as the distinct business entity held out to clients and the public, generally including any units operating under common ownership with shared resources, and that definition must be applied consistently rather than redrawn narrowly whenever it would be convenient to exclude weaker composites from firm-wide compliance. Allowing the firm to redefine itself however produces the best marketing outcome is tempting because it sounds like a business decision within the company''s control, but GIPS specifically guards against this kind of selective redefinition, since it would defeat firm-wide compliance''s purpose. Claiming that GIPS is assessed only composite by composite, with no firm-level definition at all, is also tempting because composites are indeed the unit performance is reported in, but the fundamentals of compliance require firm-wide compliance built on a consistent definition of the firm, not a composite-only framework with no firm concept at all.'),
  ('ET49', 'Ethics', 'GIPS', 'Verification', 'A firm claiming GIPS compliance engages an independent third party to perform GIPS verification. After completing the engagement, the verifier issues a report confirming the firm''s compliance. Based on this verification report alone, a prospective client can conclude that:', '["The firm has established policies and procedures designed to calculate and present performance in compliance with GIPS on a firm-wide basis, but the verification does not by itself confirm the accuracy of any specific composite''s performance presentation","Every individual composite''s performance figures presented by the firm have been confirmed as fully accurate by the verifier","Verification is legally required before any firm may claim GIPS compliance at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'GIPS verification is an optional, third-party process that tests whether a firm has complied with GIPS requirements on a firm-wide basis, including its policies and procedures for composite construction and performance presentation, but verification alone does not confirm the accuracy of any specific composite''s performance presentation, which would instead require a separate, specific performance examination. Assuming verification confirms every composite''s figures as accurate is tempting because verification sounds like a thorough audit of the numbers themselves, but it is instead focused on firm-wide processes rather than validating each individual composite''s calculations. Believing verification is a mandatory prerequisite to claiming compliance is also tempting because verification signals credibility, but GIPS compliance can be claimed without undergoing verification, since verification is an optional additional step a firm may choose to pursue.'),
  ('ET50', 'Ethics', 'GIPS', 'Claim of compliance', 'A firm has adopted most, but not all, of the required GIPS provisions for composite construction and performance calculation across its business. Under GIPS fundamentals of compliance, this firm may:', '["Claim full GIPS compliance immediately, since substantial progress toward the requirements is treated as equivalent to full compliance","Not claim GIPS compliance at all until it has met every applicable requirement; a firm cannot claim partial or selective compliance with GIPS","Claim compliance only for the specific provisions it has already adopted, describing itself as partially GIPS compliant"]'::jsonb, 1, 'GIPS fundamentals of compliance require a firm to meet all applicable requirements before making any claim of compliance; there is no recognized status of partial or selective compliance, so a firm that has adopted most but not all requirements may not yet claim compliance at all. Treating substantial progress as good enough is tempting because meeting most requirements does reflect real effort, but GIPS does not offer credit for partial adoption when it comes to the claim of compliance itself. Describing the firm as partially compliant is also tempting as it sounds like an honest, hedged description, but GIPS does not recognize a partial-compliance claim; compliance is an all-or-nothing claim once a firm chooses to make it.');
